data: {"type": "action", "id": "act15", "icon": "notebook", "title": "Coding", "results": [], "results_units": "Results", "started_at": "2026-08-01T09:14:33.863208Z", "ended_at": "2026-08-01T09:15:13.589148Z", "input": {"source": "\n# ========== CROSS-COMPETITOR COMPARISON ==========\n\nadd_heading1(doc, \"Cross-Competitor Comparison\")\nadd_hr(doc)\n\nadd_big_table(doc,\n    [\"Competitor\", \"Primary Hook\", \"AI Positioning\", \"Trust Mechanism\", \"CTA Type\"],\n    [\n        [\"HubSpot\", \"Growth / Outcomes\", \"AI as enabler\", \"Customer base, ease of use\", \"Low-friction (Get Started)\"],\n        [\"Salesforce Agentforce\", \"AI Agents / Automation\", \"AI as the product\", \"Brand authority, enterprise scale\", \"Demo / Build\"],\n        [\"Hightouch\", \"Marketing execution speed\", \"AI as brand-aware marketer\", \"Gartner Leader, named logos, metrics\", \"Demo\"],\n        [\"6sense\", \"Buyer intelligence / Why\", \"AI as interpretation layer\", \"Category ownership, GTM authority\", \"Demo\"],\n        [\"Claude\", \"Thinking and writing\", \"AI as calm assistant\", \"Safety, simplicity, trust\", \"Try (frictionless)\"],\n        [\"DOJO (opportunity)\", \"Compounding intelligence\", \"AI as system that learns your brand\", \"Graph + loop, category creation\", \"TBD\"],\n    ]\n)\n\ndoc.add_page_break()\n\n# ========== US vs EU ==========\n\nadd_heading1(doc, \"US vs. EU Website Conventions\")\nadd_hr(doc)\nadd_body(doc, \"A consistent pattern emerges when comparing how US-headquartered and EU-headquartered companies approach website design and messaging.\")\n\nadd_heading2(doc, \"What US Companies Do Better\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Extreme clarity in messaging.** Headlines are short, outcome-focused, and interpretable in under five seconds without background knowledge.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Strong category positioning.** US companies tend to name and own a category explicitly. They do not describe what they do — they define what they are.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Minimal cognitive load above the fold.** One headline, one subheadline, one CTA. Everything else is below the scroll.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Outcome-first storytelling.** The conversation starts with what changes for the customer, not how the product works.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Frictionless first actions.** CTAs like 'Try for free' or 'Get started' are lower commitment than 'Request a demo,' reducing the barrier to engagement.\")\n\nadd_heading2(doc, \"What EU Companies Often Do Better\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Technical depth and specificity.** EU company websites tend to explain their product architecture, methodology, and technical credibility in more detail.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Detailed product explanation.** Buyers who want to understand how something works before they engage find EU-style sites more satisfying.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Credibility through specificity.** Rather than relying on brand authority, EU sites build trust through the rigour and precision of their product explanation.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Clearer 'how it works' narratives.** Step-by-step or system-level explanations are more common — valuable for complex B2B products.\")\n\nadd_heading2(doc, \"The Recommendation for DOJO\")\nadd_body(doc, \"DOJO should aim to combine both approaches: US-style clarity at the top of the page, EU-style depth as the visitor scrolls. The mental model is:\")\nadd_blockquote(doc, \"Simple enough to understand in 5 seconds. Deep enough to believe in 5 minutes.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"Concretely, this means:\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Above the fold:** one clear outcome, one category claim, one CTA. No jargon, no product architecture, no feature list.\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Mid-page:** the compounding intelligence system explained in plain language, with a logical sequence (input → graph → agents → outcome → feedback loop).\")\nadd_bullet(doc, \"**Lower page:** social proof (logos, metrics, case studies), technical depth (the DOJO Graph, integration ecosystem), and trust signals (security, awards).\")\n\ndoc.add_page_break()\n\n# ========== CONSOLIDATED RECOMMENDATIONS ==========\n\nadd_heading1(doc, \"Consolidated Recommendations for DOJO's Website\")\nadd_hr(doc)\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"1. Define the category, do not describe the product.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"Every strong competitor in this analysis either owns a category (6sense: revenue intelligence; HubSpot: connected customer platform) or is actively creating one (Salesforce: agentic AI; Hightouch: agentic marketing). DOJO's category claim — 'the intelligent marketing system' — is strong. It needs to be the first thing a visitor sees and it needs to be used consistently throughout the site.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"2. Lead with the outcome of compounding, not the mechanism.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"'Compounding intelligence' is DOJO's most defensible differentiator, but the word 'compounding' requires explanation. The website should show what compounding produces: sharper decisions over time, a system that gets better the longer it runs, marketing that builds on itself rather than resetting. Show the outcome first, explain the mechanism second.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"3. Make the contrast with Hightouch visible.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"As Hightouch pivots into brand-aware AI marketing, the surface similarity to DOJO's positioning increases. The website should include a clear, confident statement about what makes DOJO different from AI tools that activate data: DOJO builds a living graph of your entire marketing reality and grounds every agent action in that context. Hightouch activates data. DOJO accumulates intelligence. That distinction should be explicit.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"4. Add specific outcome metrics to social proof.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"Logo walls are table stakes. Hightouch shows 'Otrium cuts campaign launch time by 70%.' HubSpot shows customer growth numbers. DOJO's social proof should include specific, measurable outcomes from real customers. If these do not exist yet, the website should at least frame what kinds of outcomes are possible and in what timeframe.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"5. Simplify the above-the-fold section.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"Apply the US principle: one headline, one subheadline, one CTA. Everything that cannot be understood in five seconds should be moved below the fold.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"6. Build an explicit 'how the loop works' section.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"The compounding loop — data in, graph builds, agents act, outcomes feed back, system learns — is DOJO's structural advantage and it is currently undercommunicated. A visual or sequential explanation of this loop mid-page would do significant work in differentiating DOJO from tools that simply automate tasks without learning.\")\n\nadd_heading3(doc, \"7. Address the 'why not just use Claude?' objection.\")\nadd_body(doc, \"General AI tools are increasingly the default for marketing teams on a budget. DOJO's website should pre-empt the comparison by making clear that generic AI does not know your brand, your positioning, your history, or your competitive context — and that DOJO does. This is not a subtle point. It should be stated directly.\")\n\nadd_hr(doc)\n\n# Footer note\np = doc.add_paragraph()\np.paragraph_format.space_before = Pt(12)\nrun = p.add_run(\"Analysis prepared by DOJO AI intern team, June 2026. Based on live website review as of June 23, 2026.\")\nrun.italic = True\nrun.font.size = Pt(9)\nrun.font.color.rgb = RGBColor(0x88, 0x88, 0x88)\nrun.font.name = 'Calibri'\n\n# Save\ndoc.save(\"DOJO Website Competitor Analysis.docx\")\nprint(\"Document saved successfully\")\n"}}

